    "q-129958": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129958",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [0,\\pi ].$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(\\pi -0){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(e-1)[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(e-1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A=\\pi +2(e-1)=2e+\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
