    "q-71991": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71991",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(1-3{x}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,1].$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(1-3{x}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}2y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first integral is the double integral of unity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(R)=2\\cdot 1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(1-0){\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{3}-(-1)^{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}2y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(1-3{x}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A=2-2+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
